 //=> ['    1 kB', '   10 kB', '  100 kB']
 ```
 
+### parseBytes(string)
+
+Parses a byte string back into a number of bytes.
+
+Supports plain numbers (`'512'`), bytes (`'512 B'`), the binary units `KiB`, `MiB`, `GiB`, `TiB`, `PiB`, `EiB`, `ZiB`, and `YiB`, and the units without an `i` (`kB`, `KB`, `MB`, `GB`, `TB`, `PB`, `EB`, `ZB`, and `YB`). Fractional values are allowed, the space before the unit is optional, and a leading `+` or `-` is accepted. Units are case-sensitive.
+
+All units are read as powers of 1024, so `1 MB` is `1048576`, matching how sizes are shown by Windows Explorer. This differs from `prettyBytes`, which uses powers of 1000 for `kB`, `MB`, … unless `binary` is set, so `parseBytes(prettyBytes(x))` does not round-trip.
+
+Invalid input or an unknown unit throws a `SyntaxError`. A non-string throws a `TypeError`.
+
+```js
+import {parseBytes} from 'pretty-bytes';
+
+parseBytes('512');
+//=> 512
+
+parseBytes('1.5 KiB');
+//=> 1536
+
+parseBytes('1 MB');
+//=> 1048576
+```
+
